WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
How can one cut at a membrane send one signal inward while keeping another at the

2
00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.440
surface? Follow a single plasma membrane closely around one cell.

3
00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:15.040
The answer depends on where each product can go after the cut.

4
00:00:18.483 --> 00:00:23.923
The starting block is PIP2, a lipid in the inner leaflet of the plasma membrane.

5
00:00:25.303 --> 00:00:30.063
Its oily tails stay buried, while its charged head faces the watery cytosol.

6
00:00:31.283 --> 00:00:34.883
The same lipid serves as an address label and a stored signal.

7
00:00:36.343 --> 00:00:38.663
Phospholipase C approaches from inside the cell.

8
00:00:39.667 --> 00:00:43.607
The enzyme cuts between the head and the glycerol carrying the tails.

9
00:00:44.967 --> 00:00:47.947
Both products begin at the same tiny patch of membrane.

10
00:00:49.227 --> 00:00:50.947
Which product can leave the bilayer?

11
00:00:52.347 --> 00:00:55.187
Choose the phosphate-rich head or the oily pair of tails.

12
00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:06.190
IP3 carries three phosphates, so it dissolves in cytosol and moves away.

13
00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:11.190
DAG keeps two oily tails, so it stays in the inner leaflet.

14
00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:16.970
Their different chemistry creates a moving branch and a local membrane branch.

15
00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:21.750
One cut has created two second messengers in different places.

16
00:01:22.933 --> 00:01:25.393
Different receptors can start the same cut.

17
00:01:26.753 --> 00:01:31.833
A G-protein-coupled receptor activates Gq, which activates PLC-beta.

18
00:01:33.213 --> 00:01:39.433
A receptor tyrosine kinase displays phosphorylated tyrosines, where SH2 domains dock

19
00:01:39.433 --> 00:01:45.813
PLC-gamma. Docking places PLC-gamma beside the receptor kinase.

20
00:01:46.913 --> 00:01:50.593
The kinase phosphorylates it and releases its internal brake.

21
00:01:51.433 --> 00:01:56.033
Both enzymes then make the same cut, producing IP3 and DAG.

22
00:01:57.283 --> 00:02:05.403
Now follow IP3. It crosses the cytosol and binds an IP3 receptor in the endoplasmic

23
00:02:05.403 --> 00:02:09.363
reticulum. That receptor is a calcium channel.

24
00:02:10.523 --> 00:02:14.163
IP3 does not carry calcium; it opens the route out.

25
00:02:14.163 --> 00:02:20.043
The channel opens, and green calcium dots flow from the store into cytosol.

26
00:02:21.083 --> 00:02:25.523
Researchers tested whether IP3 was sufficient without a hormone.

27
00:02:26.763 --> 00:02:30.343
They made a cell surface leaky while internal stores stayed sealed.

28
00:02:31.643 --> 00:02:33.483
No outside hormone was present.

29
00:02:34.883 --> 00:02:38.863
Added IP3 released calcium from a nonmitochondrial store.

30
00:02:40.163 --> 00:02:44.123
That separation of cause from hormone made IP3 a true messenger.

31
00:02:45.503 --> 00:02:49.423
Later work identified the store as endoplasmic reticulum.

32
00:02:50.433 --> 00:02:52.273
Return to the original cell.

33
00:02:53.573 --> 00:02:57.413
The moving branch is IP3, which opens the calcium channel.

34
00:02:58.673 --> 00:03:01.693
The local branch is DAG, still marking the membrane.

35
00:03:03.193 --> 00:03:10.873
Keep this rule: one PIP2 cut sends IP3 away and leaves DAG at the membrane.
